      ---
      #! Aquifers
      ###! EMSC3025/6025
      ---

      ###! _Dr. Sia Ghelichkhan_


# Objectives

.align{center}
  .container width:{75%}
    .box title:{By the end of this lecture, you should be able to:} background:{.lightblue}
      **Aquifer Classification:**
      - Define **aquifers** as the fundamental unit of groundwater hydrology
      - Distinguish between **unconfined**, **confined**, and **perched** aquifers
      - Classify confining beds: **aquifuge**, **aquiclude**, **aquitard**, and real-world **aquitardifers**

      **Aquifer Properties:**
      - Calculate **transmissivity** ($ T = Kb $) and **storativity** ($ S $)
      - Explain **specific storage**, **specific yield**, and **specific retention**
      - Compare storage mechanisms in confined vs unconfined aquifers

# Objectives
.align{center}
    .container width:{75%}
        .box title:{By the end of this lecture, you should be able to:} background:{.lightblue}
            **Aquifer Systems:**
            - Identify major aquifer types: **alluvial**, **fluvial**, **semiconsolidated**, **sandstone**, **carbonate-rock**, **fractured-rock** and **volcanic** aquifers
            - Understand how depositional environments control aquifer properties
            - Recognise **karst features**: sinkholes, solution conduits, and cave systems
            - Apply these concepts to Australia's groundwater provinces, from the Great Artesian Basin to the Perth Basin and the Northern Territory carbonates

# Introduction

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    - Just as the **catchment** is the basic unit of surface hydrology, the **aquifer** is the fundamental unit of groundwater hydrology.
    - Aquifers are defined by their hydraulic properties rather than strictly by geology.
    - Understanding aquifer types and properties is crucial for:
      - **Groundwater management**
      - **Well design and sustainability**
      - **Resource assessment**

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Key concept**

      An aquifer's definition depends on context—what's considered an aquifer in one region might be a confining bed in another.

# Defining an Aquifer

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      ###! An aquifer is "a lithologic unit or combination of lithologic units capable of yielding water to pumped wells or springs"
      — Domenico (1972)

    - An aquifer can be:
      - Coextensive with **geologic formations**
      - A **group of formations**
      - **Part of a formation**
      - Independent of geologic units (cutting across formations)

  .container width:{40%}
    !(100%)[Geologic formations in Grand Canyon](../image/internet_grand_canyon.jpg "Geologic formations can be very complex, and Aquifers can be coextensive with them.")

# Confining Beds

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - Units of **low permeability** that bound an aquifer are called **confining beds**.
    - The designation is **context-dependent**:
      - In areas with prolific aquifers → low permeability unit = confining bed
      - In groundwater-poor regions → same deposit might be considered an aquifer

  .container width:{50%} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred} radius:{0.5cm}
      **Important note**

      In practice, this ambiguity is resolved by explicitly defining **hydraulic conductivity** or **porosity** values that characterise the unit.
      !(100%)[Confining Beds](../image/internet_schematic_confiningbed.jpg "Confining Beds")



# Unconfined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - Also called **water table aquifers**
    - The **water table** forms the upper boundary
    - No confining layer above
    - Wells or piezometers installed in unconfined aquifers approximately define the water table position
    - Water is under **atmospheric pressure** at the water table

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Unconfined aquifer](../image/franklin_fig_4-1.png "Unconfined, confined, and perched aquifers")

# Confined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - Also called **artesian aquifers**
    - Bounded by confining beds on **both top and bottom**
    - Water is under **pressure** greater than atmospheric
    - The water level in a well occurs **above the upper boundary** of the aquifer
    - Occasionally, water level occurs **above ground surface** (flowing artesian well)

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Confined aquifer](../image/franklin_fig_4-1.png "Unconfined, confined, and perched aquifers")

# Perched Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - An **unconfined aquifer** that develops **above the regional water table**
    - Forms on top of a low hydraulic conductivity layer
    - An **unsaturated zone exists below** the perched aquifer
    - Often temporary or seasonal
    - Important for local water supply in some areas

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Perched aquifer](../image/franklin_fig_4-1.png "Unconfined, confined, and perched aquifers")

    **Key feature**: Unsaturated zone both above and below the perched zone

# Comparing Aquifer Types

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    |**Aquifer Type**|**Upper Boundary**|**Lower Boundary**|**Pressure**|**Water Level**|
    |:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
    |**Unconfined**|Water table (free surface)|Confining bed or impermeable layer|Atmospheric at water table|At water table|
    |**Confined**|Confining bed|Confining bed|Above atmospheric|Above aquifer top|
    |**Perched**|Water table|Low-K layer (perching layer)|Atmospheric at water table|Above regional water table|
    "Summary of aquifer types and their characteristics"

#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred} alignment:{center}
    ##! Types of Confining Beds

.whitespace height:{3cm}

# Different Classifications of Confining Beds

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container width:{50%}
    Three terms are occasionally used to describe different types of confining beds:

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
      **Aquifuge**

      - The ultimate low hydraulic conductivity unit
      - **Extremely poor** conductor of groundwater
      - **No storage capacity**
      - Example: intact unfractured crystalline rock

  .container width:{50%}
    .center
        .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
          **Aquiclude**

          - Similarly **low permeability** as aquifuge
          - **Able to store water** (has porosity)
          - Transmits water very slowly
          - Example: clay layers
    .whitespace height:{0.2cm}

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Aquitard**

      - A **low permeability unit** (leaky confining bed)
      - Can **store and transmit** water between adjacent aquifers
      - Stored water available to wells in nearby aquifers
      - Example: silty clay layers

# The Role of Aquitards

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    Aquitards play important dual roles:

    - **Influence rates of recharge** between aquifer layers
    - Can **protect underlying aquifers** from contaminants migrating downward from the ground surface
    - Act as a **barrier** to vertical contaminant transport
    - Provide **slow release** of water to adjacent aquifers during pumping

  .container width:{40%}
    !(100%)[Aquitard between two aquifers](../image/internet_aquitard.jpg "Aquitard between two aquifers")

# The Complexity of Real Aquitards

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - Detailed characterisation in the **midcontinent region of North America** shows that simple concepts don't capture reality.
    - Runkel et al. (2018) use the term **"aquitardifers"** to describe confining beds that behave **both as an aquitard and aquifer** at the same place.

  .container width:{50%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred} radius:{0.5cm}
      **Key insight**

      Real confining beds are often more complex than simple one-dimensional models suggest.

# Characteristics of Aquitardifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    **Extreme anisotropy** in hydraulic conductivity:

    - **Horizontal direction**:
      - Permeable partings **parallel to bedding**
      - Can transmit water horizontally like an aquifer

    - **Vertical direction**:
      - Layering of low permeability units
      - Sufficiently low $ K_v $ to inhibit vertical transport of contaminants

  .container width:{40%} alignment:{center}
    !(100%)[Aquitardifers](../image/internet_horizontal_flow_aquitard.png "Aquitardifers")

# Additional Complexity: Fractures

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    In some shallow settings, aquitardifers develop **vertical fractures**:

    - Create **fast-flow pathways**
    - Can extend over **relatively large areas**
    - Provide direct connection between surface and deeper units
    - Completely change the protective function of the confining bed
    - Challenge simple predictions of contaminant transport

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Management implication**

      Detailed site characterisation is essential—simple textbook models may not apply.

#
.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
    ##! Key Aquifer Properties


.whitespace height:{2cm}

# Two Important Aquifer Properties

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    When a pump is turned on in a well:

    1. Water level in the well casing (and hydraulic head) is **reduced**
    2. This causes groundwater to **flow from the aquifer into the well**
    3. Much of the pumped water initially comes from **"storage"** in the aquifer

    Therefore, aquifers have at least two important characteristics:
    - **Ability to store** groundwater
    - **Ability to transmit** this water to a nearby well

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Key concept**

      These properties depend to an important extent on the **geologic setting**.

# Transmissivity

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Definition**

      The rate at which water of prevailing kinematic viscosity is transmitted through a **unit width** of the aquifer under a **unit hydraulic gradient**.

    - Similar concept to hydraulic conductivity
    - Main difference: applies across the **vertical thickness** of an aquifer
    - Integrates conductivity over aquifer thickness

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Transmissivity concept](../image/franklin_fig_4-2.png "Transmissivity and Conductivity")

# Transmissivity Equation

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    If the thickness of the aquifer is $ b $, the transmissivity $ T $ is:

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{2pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center} radius:{0.5cm}
        $ T = K \times b $

    Where:
    - $ T $ = transmissivity $ [L^2/T] $
    - $ K $ = hydraulic conductivity $ [L/T] $
    - $ b $ = aquifer thickness $ [L] $

    **Units**: m²/day, ft²/day, or gpd/ft (gallons per day per foot)

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Physical meaning**

      Transmissivity tells you how much water can flow through the **entire thickness** of an aquifer per unit width.

# Darcy's Law for Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    For a **homogeneous confined aquifer**, Darcy's equation can be written in terms of transmissivity:

    !(100%)[Transmissivity diagram](../image/franklin_fig_3-7.png)

  .container width:{50%}
    Apply Darcy's law across the aquifer:

    $ Q = T \times W \times i $

    Where:
    - $ Q $ = discharge \[ $ L^3/T $ \]
    - $ T $ = transmissivity \[ $ L^2/T $ \]
    - $ W $ = width perpendicular to flow \[ $ L $ \]
    - $ i $ = hydraulic gradient [dimensionless]


# Storativity and Specific Storage

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    Aquifers can store water. **How this storage is accomplished differs** depending upon whether the aquifer is confined or unconfined.

    **Confined aquifer — Two mechanisms supply water**:

    1. **Water expansion**: Lower pressure → water molecules expand slightly
    2. **Aquifer compression**: Lower water pressure → less support for skeleton → grains pushed closer together → squeezes water out

  .container width:{40%}
    !(100%)[Confined aquifers and pumping](../image/internet_unconfined_aquifer.png "Confined aquifer before and after pumping")

# Understanding Aquifer Compression

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{40%}
    **Before pumping**:
    - Water pressure holds grains apart
    - Aquifer skeleton is supported
    - Pore space is at maximum

    **During pumping (head declines)**:
    - Water pressure decreases
    - Weight of overlying rocks pushes down
    - Grains move closer together
    - Pore space reduces → water squeezed out

    **Result**: Only **small amounts** of water released ($ S = 10^{-5} $ to $ 10^{-3} $)

  .container width:{60%}
    !(100%)[Aquifer](../image/internet_AquiferCompaction_508Blue_crop_4mg_0.gif "Aquifer compaction")


# Land Subsidence: Permanent Compression

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    When pumping is **excessive or prolonged**:
    - Initial compression is **elastic** (recoverable)
    - Continued compression becomes **plastic** (permanent)
    - Aquifer structure permanently altered
    - Land surface subsides

    **Why clay layers are critical**:
    - Clay is ~1000× more compressible than rock
    - Major contributor to subsidence
    - Once compressed, **cannot recover**

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Real-world example**

      **Latrobe Valley, Victoria**:
      - Open-cut coal mines pump the confined Latrobe Group aquifers
      - Heads fell 120 to 130 m at the mines
      - Land subsided **1 to 2.2 m** near the mines, from compaction of coal and clay

# Storage in Unconfined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    **Unconfined aquifer**:
    - Main source of water is **drainage of water from pores** as the water table declines
    - Much more water released per unit decline in head
    - For a comparable unit decline in hydraulic head, an unconfined aquifer releases **much more water** from storage than a confined aquifer

  .container width:{40%}
    !(100%)[Unconfined aquifer and pumping](../image/internet_schematic_cross_sectional_diagram_744.webp  "Unconfined aquifer with water table dropping, water draining from pores above the new water table level")

# Storativity Definition

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    .container borderwidth:{2pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
      **Storativity** ($ S $) is the volume of water that an aquifer releases from or takes into storage per **unit surface area** of the aquifer per **unit change in head**.

    .align{center}
      $ S = \frac{\text{volume of water}}{\text{unit area} \times \text{unit head change}} = \frac{m^3}{m^2 \times m} $

    - Dimensionless quantity
    - For **confined aquifers**: $ S $ ranges between $ 10^{-3} $ to $ 10^{-5} $
    - For **unconfined aquifers**: $ S $ (or specific yield) ranges between $ 0.01 $ to $ 0.30 $

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Storativity concept](../image/franklin_fig_4-3.png "Concept of storativity and specific storage")

# Specific Storage

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Specific storage** ($ S_s $) is the volume of water that an aquifer releases from or takes into storage per **unit volume** of aquifer per **unit change in head**.

    .align{center}
      $ S_s = \frac{\text{volume of water}}{\text{unit volume} \times \text{unit head change}} $

    Units: $ [1/L] $ (e.g., $ 1/m $ or $ 1/ft $)

  .container width:{40%}
    **Relationship between $ S $ and $ S_s $:**

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{2pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
        $ S = S_s \times b $

    where $ b $ is the aquifer thickness.

# Visual Summary: Storativity

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    !(62%)[Storativity diagram](../image/franklin_fig_4-3.png "Concept of storativity: confined aquifer shows small release due to water expansion and aquifer compression; unconfined aquifer shows large release due to drainage from pores")

# Comparing Storage Properties

.row gap:{1cm}
  .center
     .container width:{100%} alignment:{center}
       .center
           |**Property**|**Symbol**|**Definition**|**Units**|**Typical Values**|
           |:---|:---:|:---|:---:|:---|
           |**Specific Storage**|$ S_s $|Storage per unit volume|$ [1/L] $|$ 10^{-6} $ to $ 10^{-4} $ (1/m)|
           |**Storativity (confined)**|$ S $|Storage per unit area|Dimensionless|$ 10^{-5} $ to $ 10^{-3} $|
           |**Specific Yield (unconfined)**|$ S_y $|Drainable porosity|Dimensionless|$ 0.01 $ to $ 0.30 $|
           "Summary of aquifer storage properties"

#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred} alignment:{center}
    ##! Mathematical Formulation of Storage

.whitespace height:{3cm}

# Specific Storage in Confined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    For a confined aquifer, specific storage reflects storage from:
    - **Compression of the granular matrix**
    - **Expansion of water**

    The mathematical definition (Domenico and Schwartz, 1998):

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{2pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center} radius:{0.5cm}
        $ S_s = \rho_w g (\beta_p + n\beta_w) $

  .container width:{40%}
    Where:
    - $ \rho_w $ = density of water $ [ML^{-3}] $
    - $ g $ = gravitational constant (9.81 m/sec²) $ [LT^{-2}] $
    - $ n $ = porosity of the aquifer
    - $ \beta_p $ = vertical compressibility of rock matrix
    - $ \beta_w $ = compressibility of water

# Compressibility of Water and Matrix

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    **Compressibility** ($ \beta $) quantifies how much a material compresses under pressure.

    **Units**: inverse of pressure \[$ 1/Pa $\] or \[$ m^2/N $\]

    **Key insight**:
    - **Higher** compressibility = more compressible
    - **Lower** compressibility = more rigid/incompressible

    Water compressibility ($ \beta_w $):
    - $ 4.4 \times 10^{-10} $ m²/N at 25°C

  .container width:{50%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Material property comparison**

      Clay is ~1000× more compressible than sound rock

      This is why clay layers are major contributors to land subsidence!

# Compressibility Values for Earth Materials

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{85%}
      **Geologic Materials (matrix compressibility $ \alpha $)**

      | Material | Compressibility (m²/N) |
      |:---------|:----------------------:|
      | Clay | $ 1 \times 10^{-6} $ to $ 1 \times 10^{-8} $ |
      | Sand | $ 1 \times 10^{-7} $ to $ 1 \times 10^{-9} $ |
      | Gravel | $ 1 \times 10^{-8} $ to $ 1 \times 10^{-10} $ |
      | Jointed Rock | $ 1 \times 10^{-8} $ to $ 1 \times 10^{-10} $ |
      | Sound Rock | $ 1 \times 10^{-9} $ to $ 1 \times 10^{-11} $ |

      .whitespace height:{0.5cm}

      **Fluid compressibility $ \beta $**

      | Fluid | Compressibility (m²/N) |
      |:------|:----------------------:|
      | Water | $ 4.4 \times 10^{-10} $ |

      _Note: Loose sediments are more compressible than well-cemented rocks_

# Using the Specific Storage Equation

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    The equation $ S_s = \rho_w g (\beta_p + n\beta_w) $ can be used to estimate specific storage and storativity.

    **Example calculation**:
    - Clay layer with $ n = 0.3 $
    - $ \beta_p = 1 \times 10^{-7} $ m²/N
    - $ \beta_w = 4.4 \times 10^{-10} $ m²/N
    - $ \rho_w g \approx 10^4 $ N/m³

    $ S_s = 10^4 (1 \times 10^{-7} + 0.3 \times 4.4 \times 10^{-10}) $
    $ S_s \approx 1 \times 10^{-3} $ m⁻¹

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Key observation**

      The matrix compressibility ($ \beta_p $) term typically **dominates** over the water compressibility term, especially in fine-grained materials like clay.

# Storage in Unconfined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    In an unconfined aquifer, the groundwater response to pumping is **different** from a confined aquifer:

    **Early time** (no significant water level change):
    - Water comes from **expansion** of the water
    - Water comes from **compression** of the grains
    - Similar to confined aquifer behaviors

    **Later on** (water table falls):
    - Water mainly comes from **gravity drainage** of pores
    - This is the dominant storage mechanism

  .container width:{40%}
    !(100%)[Unconfined aquifer](../image/internet_b.png "Response to reduction in hydraulic head")

# Storativity of Unconfined Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    The storativity of an unconfined aquifer is expressed as:

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{2pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center} radius:{0.5cm}
        $ S = S_y + bS_s $

    Where:
    - $ S_y $ = specific yield of the aquifer
    - $ b $ = aquifer thickness
    - $ S_s $ = specific storage

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Key insight**

      - Specific yield: $ 0.1 $ to $ 0.3 $
      - Product $ bS_s $: $ 10^{-3} $ to $ 10^{-5} $

      Thus, **specific yield is the dominant storage term** for unconfined aquifers.

# Aquifer Type Transitions

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    In some cases, an aquifer may be:
    - **Confined at early stage** of pumping
    - **Becomes unconfined** at late time

    **Process**:
    1. Initial pumping creates cone of depression
    2. Water levels decline
    3. Head falls below the top of the aquifer
    4. Aquifer dewaters from the top down
    5. Storage mechanism changes from elastic to gravity drainage

    As the aquifer changes from confined to unconfined, **storativity values change accordingly**.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Aquifer type transition](../image/franklin_fig_4-1.png "Aquifers can transition from confined to unconfined")

# Specific Yield and Specific Retention

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Specific yield** ($ S_y $) is the water released from a water-bearing material by **gravity drainage**.

      Say the ratio of the volume of water yielded from a soil or rock by gravity drainage after being saturated, to the total volume of the soil or rock.

    Expressed as the ratio of:

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
        $ S_y = \frac{V_d}{V_T} $

    Where:
    - $ V_d $ = volume of water that drains by gravity
    - $ V_T $ = total volume of soil or rock (after being saturated)

  .container width:{40%}
    <!-- Placeholder: Diagram showing saturated sample before drainage and after drainage, with water draining out under gravity, showing retained water film on grains -->

# Specific Retention

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    Not all water initially present is released from storage.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Specific retention** ($ S_r $) describes water that is **retained** as a film on grain surfaces or held in small openings by molecular attraction.

    .align{center}
      .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
        $ S_r = \frac{V_r}{V_T} $

    Where:
    - $ V_r $ = volume of water retained against gravity
    - $ V_T $ = total volume of soil or rock

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Important relationship**

      Porosity is related to specific yield and specific retention by:

      $ n = S_y + S_r $

      The sum equals the total porosity.

# Specific Retention Characteristics

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    Specific retention **increases with**:
    - **Decrease in grain size** (more surface area)
    - **Decrease in pore size** (stronger capillary forces)

    **Typical values**:
    - Coarse gravel: $ S_r \approx 0.01 $ to $ 0.05 $
    - Fine sand: $ S_r \approx 0.05 $ to $ 0.15 $
    - Silt: $ S_r \approx 0.15 $ to $ 0.30 $
    - Clay: $ S_r \approx 0.30 $ to $ 0.50 $

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Inverse relationship**

      Materials with high specific retention have **low specific yield**, and vice versa.

      This is why clay has high porosity but yields little water!

#
.whitespace height:{3cm}

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
    ##! Types of Aquifer Systems

# Unconsolidated Sand and Gravel Aquifers

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    **Characteristics**:
    - Intergranular porosity
    - Primarily **unconfined** conditions
    - High permeability (varies with clay content and sorting)
    - The most heavily pumped aquifer type in Australia

    **Three main settings in Australia**:
    1. **Alluvial**: the Lachlan, Namoi, Murrumbidgee and Condamine rivers, the Lockyer Valley
    2. **Coastal sand**: the superficial aquifer under Perth, the Burdekin delta
    3. **Palaeovalley**: sand buried in old river channels, for example under the Yilgarn

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Key properties**

      - High storage and transmission
      - Thicker deposits → more regional flow
      - **Susceptible to contamination**
      - Alluvial aquifers hold about 20% of Australia's bores but give about 60% of the extraction

# Unconsolidated Aquifers Summary

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    **Valley-Fill and Palaeovalley Aquifers**:
    - Sediment that fills a valley or a buried river channel
    - Long, narrow bodies with interbedded materials
    - Examples: the Condamine and Lockyer valleys (Qld)
    - Yilgarn palaeochannel sands: $ K $ = 20–70 m/day, water more than 100 000 years old

    **Fluvial Aquifers**:
    - Deposited by river systems
    - Two types: meandering vs braided
    - Braided → more continuous and productive
    - Long, narrow, thin geometry
    - Examples: the Shepparton and Calivil formations of the Murray–Darling rivers

  .container width:{50%}
    **Key characteristics**:
    - High permeability (varies with sorting)
    - Unconfined conditions
    - High specific yield ($ 0.1-0.3 $)
    - Susceptible to contamination
    - Most heavily pumped in Australia

    **Settings**:
    - Alluvial
    - Coastal sand
    - Palaeovalley

# Consolidated Aquifers Summary

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    **Semiconsolidated Sediments**:
    - Layered sand, clay and limestone in young basins
    - Coastal wedges thicken toward the sea: unconfined where they crop out, confined further down dip
    - Examples: the Murray Basin, the Gippsland Basin, Bundaberg
    - Risk: saltwater intrusion (Bundaberg, about 12 500 ha lost)
    - Risk: land subsidence (Latrobe Valley, 1 to 2.2 m)

    **Sandstone Aquifers**:
    - Cemented sand with reduced porosity
    - Flow in fractures and bedding planes
    - Low to moderate hydraulic conductivity
    - Large areal extent
    - Example: the Great Artesian Basin, recharged on its eastern outcrop at about 15 mm/yr

  .container width:{50%}
    **Carbonate-Rock Aquifers**:
    - Limestone and dolostone
    - Solution-enhanced permeability
    - **10 orders of magnitude** range in $ K $
    - Large yields where well-connected
    - Examples: the Tindall Limestone (NT), the Gambier Limestone (SA)

    **Other Types**:
    - **Crystalline-rock**: fractured granite and metasediments, flow in fractures
    - **Volcanic-rock**: basalt with cooling fractures and lava tubes

# Carbonate Aquifer Characteristics

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    **Composition**:
    - **Calcite** (CaCO₃) → limestone
    - **Dolomite** (CaMg(CO₃)₂) → dolostone
    - Marine origin (shells, corals, algae, precipitates)

    **Solution openings range from**:
    - Small tubes and widened joints
    - To caverns **tens of meters wide**
    - Extending **hundreds to thousands of meters** in length

    **Hydraulic properties**:
    - Well-connected networks → **large yields** to wells
    - Undissolved rock between openings → nearly impermeable
    - Extreme heterogeneity

  .container width:{40%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Australian example**

      **Tindall Limestone**, Katherine (NT): $ T $ reaches 17 000 m²/day, and dye moved 570 to 1250 m/day from a sinkhole to a spring. Its discharge keeps the Katherine and Daly rivers flowing through the dry season.

# Karst Systems Summary

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{60%}
    **Formation requirements**:
    1. Chemically aggressive groundwater
    2. Fractures present for transmission
    3. Groundwater can drain out

    **Solution features**:
    - Start as small conduits (~1 cm)
    - Grow to cave size (kilometers long)
    - Highest yields at fracture intersections
    - Extremely heterogeneous permeability

  .container width:{40%}
    **Karst landforms**:
    - Sinkholes (dissolution, cover subsidence, cover-collapse)
    - Stream sinks (disappearing streams)
    - Dry valleys
    - Large spring systems
    - Underground cave networks

    **Australian examples**:
    - The Nullarbor: the largest area of exposed karst in the world
    - Mount Gambier: sinkholes and cenotes
    - Mataranka springs (NT)

# Other Important Aquifer Types

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    **Crystalline-rock aquifers**:
    - Fractured granite, basalt, metamorphic rocks
    - Very low primary porosity
    - Flow occurs **primarily in fractures**
    - Secondary porosity controls productivity
    - Examples: the Yilgarn (WA), the Mount Lofty Ranges (SA), the eastern highlands
    - Well yields highly variable

  .container width:{50%}
    **Volcanic-rock aquifers**:
    - Basalt flows with **high permeability**
    - Flow in cooling fractures and lava tubes
    - Vesicular zones store water
    - Examples: the Newer Volcanics (Vic), the Atherton Tablelands (Qld)
    - Can be extremely productive locally
    - Complex interbedded structure

# Comparing Major Aquifer Types

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{95%}
      |**Aquifer Type**|**Porosity**|**K Range**|**Storage**|**Key Feature**|**Main Challenge**|
      |:---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---|:---|
      |**Unconsolidated**|High (0.25-0.35)|High|$ S_y = 0.1-0.3 $|Intergranular|Contamination|
      |**Basin-fill**|High|High-Moderate|High|Thick accumulations|Over-exploitation|
      |**Fluvial (braided)**|High|High|High|Lateral continuity|Variable quality|
      |**Semiconsolidated**|Moderate|Moderate|Moderate|Wedge geometry|Subsidence, saltwater|
      |**Sandstone**|Low-Moderate|Low-Moderate|$ S = 10^{-3}-10^{-5} $|Fracture flow|Vertical leakage|
      |**Carbonate**|Variable|10 orders of magnitude|Variable|Solution conduits|Heterogeneity|
      |**Karst**|Highly variable|Extremely high locally|Low-Moderate|Caves and conduits|Rapid transport|
      |**Crystalline**|Very low|Very low|Very low|Fractures only|Limited yield|

      _Comparison of principal aquifer characteristics_


#
.whitespace height:{3cm}

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
  .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue} alignment:{center}
    ##! Australian Aquifer Systems

# Groundwater in Australia

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{42%}
    !(85%)[Hydrogeology of Australia](../image/ga_hydrogeology_map_1987.jpg "Hydrogeology of Australia (BMR 1987). GA, CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{58%}
    - Australia uses about 3 to 5 thousand GL of groundwater per year. The ABS counted 3,249 GL of self-extracted groundwater in 2021–22.
    - Groundwater is about one fifth to one third of all water use. The share depends on the year and on how use is counted.
    - Irrigation and stock take most of it, then mining, then town supply.
    - The Northern Territory gets about 90% of its water from aquifers, and Western Australia about two thirds.
    - In much of the arid interior, groundwater is the only reliable supply for towns, stations and mines.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue}
      Alluvial aquifers hold about 20% of all bores but give about 60% of the extraction.

# How the aquifers are classified

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Groundwater provinces](../image/internet_parlimant_GW.jpg "Groundwater provinces of Australia. The slides that follow go through them one by one.")

  .container width:{50%}
    Three newer schemes use the same idea at different scales:

    - **Geoscience Australia** uses six aquifer settings: alluvial, coastal, fractured rock, sedimentary basin, palaeovalley and karst.
    - The **National Hydrogeological Inventory** (2023) splits the continent into about 40 groundwater provinces: sedimentary basins and fractured-rock provinces.
    - The Bureau of Meteorology's **National Aquifer Framework** groups formations into hydrogeologic units and complexes, so that every state uses the same names.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      About 40% of Australia's groundwater is stored in fractured rock, but fractured rock gives only about 10% of the extraction.

# Great Artesian Basin

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    !(72%)[GAB section](../image/ga_gab_section_1987.jpg "West–east section through the Eromanga Basin. Blue: Jurassic (J) and Cretaceous (K) sandstone aquifers. Arrows: flow from the Great Dividing Range. GA 1987, CC BY 4.0.")

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{55%}
    - More than 1.7 million km², one fifth of Australia (Qld, NSW, SA, NT).
    - Jurassic–Cretaceous sandstones under the marine **Rolling Downs** mudstone, up to 3000 m thick.
    - **Confined** and **artesian**. $ K $ = 0.1 to 10 m/day, $ S \approx 10^{-5} $.

  .container width:{45%}
    - Recharge on the eastern outcrop, mean about 15 mm/yr.
    - Flow 1 to 5 m/yr. Water near the centre is more than one million years old.

# Great Artesian Basin: 140 years of flowing bores

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{55%}
    **Use**: about 450 GL/yr. Stock 41%, irrigation 25%, oil and gas 19%, more than 120 towns 9%.

    **Pressure loss**:
    - About 4700 flowing bores were drilled after the 1880s.
    - Open bore drains up to 100 km long lost more than 90% of the water to seepage and evaporation.
    - Heads fell by up to about 100 m, and many springs stopped flowing.

    **Recovery**:
    - Bore capping and piping (GABSI, 1999–2017) controlled about 750 bores and replaced 21 000 km of drains.
    - This saves about 250 GL/yr, and pressures are rising again in many areas.

  .container width:{45%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **Springs**

      More than 460 spring groups depend on GAB discharge. The mound springs near Lake Eyre are a listed endangered ecological community.

    .whitespace height:{0.4cm}

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Coal seam gas**

      Gas wells in the Surat Basin pump about 52 GL/yr of water from coal seams. Drawdown in the Walloon Coal Measures is up to 280 m.

# Murray Basin

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{45%}
    !(88%)[Murray Basin salinity](../image/ga_murray_basin_salinity.jpg "Salinity (mg/L) of the shallow unconfined aquifer. Evans and Kellett (1989), Fig. 17. Geoscience Australia, CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{55%}
    - A saucer-shaped Cenozoic basin of about 300 000 km² under NSW, Vic and SA, up to about 600 m thick.
    - Stacked porous aquifers: the confined **Renmark Group** (fluvial sand), the **Murray Group limestone**, the Pliocene sands, and the **Shepparton Formation** at the top.
    - A **closed** groundwater basin. The River Murray is the only drain for water and salt.
    - Salinity ranges from below 200 to 300 000 mg/L.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      Clearing the Mallee raised recharge from below 0.1 mm/yr to 8–40 mm/yr. Fourteen salt interception schemes now pump saline groundwater away from the river.

# Alluvial aquifers of the Murray–Darling rivers

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    !(70%)[Riverine section](../image/ga_murray_basin_section.png "Section down a flow line from the Murrumbidgee River to the River Murray. Evans and Kellett (1989), Fig. 6. GA, CC BY 4.0.")

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    - Lachlan, Namoi, Murrumbidgee, Condamine: unconsolidated sand and gravel, unconfined over confined layers.
    - Most recharge on the plains is **leakage from rivers**. Pumping turned many gaining rivers into losing rivers.

  .container width:{50%}
    - Basin groundwater use: 1832 GL in 2004–05 (16% of all water use), 1232 GL in 2023–24.
    - Lower Namoi: allocations of 213 GL/yr against a sustainable yield of 95 GL/yr. Condamine: levels down 3 to 35 m since the 1960s.

# Perth Basin

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{40%}
    !(72%)[Perth recharge](../image/dawes_perth_recharge.png "Modelled recharge (mm), six scenarios. Dawes et al. (2012), CC BY 3.0.")

  .container width:{60%}
    - A rift basin about 1300 km long. Fresh groundwater reaches about 1 km below Perth.
    - **Superficial aquifer**: unconfined sand, with karstic Tamala Limestone at the coast ($ K $ up to 100–1000 m/day). Recharge builds two water-table mounds, Gnangara and Jandakot.
    - **Leederville** and **Yarragadee**: deep, confined, layered sandstone, with water up to about 36 000 years old.
    - Groundwater supplies about 70% of all water used in Perth and Mandurah.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      Rainfall has fallen about 15% since 1975. The Gnangara Mound has lost more than 1000 GL of storage since 1980, and wetlands and cave pools have dried. Perth can now inject up to 28 GL/yr of recycled water into the confined aquifers.

# Otway Basin: Mount Gambier

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Mount Gambier section](../image/somaratne_mount_gambier_section.png "Section under Mount Gambier: Gambier Limestone over the confined Dilwyn Formation, and the Blue Lake crater. Somaratne et al. (2014), CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{50%}
    - **Gambier Limestone**: unconfined karst, with sinkholes, cenotes and spring-fed ponds. $ T $ = 450 to 24 000 m²/day.
    - **Dilwyn Formation**: confined sand below, artesian in places. Most of its water is older than 25 000 years.
    - Recharge is about 1300 GL/yr. Licensed extraction was 177 GL in 2022–23, and plantations take about 240 GL/yr more.
    - The groundwater-fed Blue Lake supplies Mount Gambier with about 3.5 GL/yr.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue}
      Plantations are licensed as groundwater users here, because their roots reach a shallow water table.

# Gippsland Basin: Latrobe Valley

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{45%}
    !(90%)[Latrobe Group heads](../image/ba_gippsland_latrobe_heads.png "Head (m AHD) in the upper Latrobe Group aquifers, 1980 and 2012. Bioregional Assessment Programme (2015), CC BY 3.0 AU.")

  .container width:{55%}
    - Confined **Latrobe Group** sands below brown coal seams up to 135 m thick. The aquifers were artesian before development.
    - The open-cut coal mines pump about 30 GL/yr to keep their floors stable. Offshore oil and gas production takes about 100 GL/yr more.
    - Heads fell 120 to 130 m at the mines and about 1 m/yr across the region.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      **Land subsidence**: 1 to 2.2 m near the mines and 0.3 to 0.5 m across the region, from compaction of coal and clay. The coast near the Gippsland Lakes fell 27 to 89 mm from 2004 to 2019.

# Newer Volcanics, western Victoria

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{68%}
    - More than 400 volcanic centres, Pliocene to Holocene, over about 19 000 km².
    - Water moves through **fractures, flow contacts and lava tubes**; scoria cones store it. $ K $ ranges from $ 10^{-3} $ to $ 10^{2} $ m/day.
    - Recharge is up to about 30 mm/yr at young volcanoes but 1 to 2 mm/yr through clay soils. Salinity rises from below 560 mg/L near vents to above 3000 mg/L on the plains.
    - Use is about 23 GL/yr. Many basalt-plain wetlands depend on groundwater.
    - The Atherton Tablelands basalt (Qld) is the same aquifer type in a wet climate: recharge 150 to 660 mm/yr, water less than 30 years old.

  .container width:{32%}
    !(68%)[Crater lakes](../image/kirono_crater_lakes.png "Crater lake depth, observed (dots) and modelled. Kirono et al. (2009), CC BY 4.0.")

# Northern Territory carbonates: the Tindall Limestone

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{52%}
    !(90%)[Tindall Limestone aquifer](../image/ntg_tindall_conceptual_model.png "Tindall Limestone aquifer at Katherine. Rooke (2024), NT Government, CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{48%}
    - Cambrian limestone and dolostone: the Tindall Limestone and Oolloo Dolostone of the Daly Basin.
    - **Karst**: $ T $ reaches 17 000 m²/day. Dye moved 570 to 1250 m/day from a sinkhole to springs.
    - About 70% of the monsoon recharge enters through sinkholes and macropores.
    - Discharge keeps the Katherine and Daly rivers flowing through the dry season.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      Licences are cut in dry years to protect river flow. Fire-fighting foam from RAAF Base Tindal put PFAS in the aquifer.

# The Cambrian Limestone Aquifer

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{34%}
    !(85%)[CLA flow systems](../image/gba_cla_flow_systems.jpg "Cambrian Limestone Aquifer flow systems. GBA Program (2020), CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{66%}
    - One karst aquifer system across the Daly, Wiso and Georgina basins, about 570 000 km².
    - Recharge follows the rainfall gradient: median about 82 mm/yr in the Daly Basin, 9 mm/yr in the Georgina Basin.
    - Flow paths hundreds of kilometres long end at springs: Mataranka (25–33 °C), the Flora River and the Roper River.
    - Water is mostly below 1000 mg/L TDS. Present use is small, but plans allow large increases.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue}
      The Beetaloo shale gas targets lie below this aquifer, separated by aquitards. How well those aquitards seal is not yet known.

# Canning Basin

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{55%}
    - The largest sedimentary basin in WA, about 430 000 km² onshore, and almost undeveloped.
    - **Broome Sandstone**: unconfined, weakly consolidated. Coastal throughflow holds the seawater interface in place.
    - **Wallal Sandstone**: confined below the Jarlemai Siltstone. On the coastal plain it is artesian, with heads 2 to 30 m above ground.
    - **Grant Group and Poole Sandstone** (Fitzroy Valley): up to 2000 m thick and artesian. Water is up to 40 000 years old.

  .container width:{45%}
    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}
      **A resource in waiting**

      Fitzroy Valley use is about 5 GL/yr. A CSIRO assessment estimates that up to 170 GL/yr could be taken. The 120 GL/yr of this from the Grant–Poole aquifer is less than 5% of its recharge.

    .whitespace height:{0.4cm}

    Groundwater discharge feeds the dry-season pools of the Fitzroy River and Mandora Marsh, which has the most inland mangroves in Australia.

# Pilbara: Millstream and mine dewatering

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{50%}
    **Millstream aquifer**
    - Cainozoic dolomite and calcrete in the Fortescue valley: karst with cavities. Storage about 1.7 km³.
    - Recharge comes mainly from **floods**. One cyclone flood in 1975 added about 49 GL.
    - Springs feed 20 km of permanent river pools. A water-table fall of only 0.23 m reduced spring flow, so pumping now follows rules on minimum levels.

  .container width:{50%}
    **Iron-ore mines**
    - Mines below the water table must dewater. In 2012 the Pilbara abstracted about 400 GL/yr: 48% for mining and 40% as surplus mine water released to creeks.
    - One mine next to the Fortescue Marsh is approved to pump up to 100 GL/yr and reinject most of it.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue}
      About 350 species of stygofauna, animals that live in groundwater, have been found in Pilbara aquifers.

# Eucla Basin: the Nullarbor

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{52%}
    !(100%)[Nullarbor Plain](../image/woodhead_nullarbor_map.jpg "The Nullarbor Plain and dated caves. Woodhead et al. (2019), Scientific Reports, CC BY 4.0.")

  .container width:{48%}
    - Eocene to Miocene marine limestones (Wilson Bluff, Abrakurrie, Nullarbor): the largest area of exposed karst in the world.
    - Rainfall is 150 to 250 mm/yr. Recharge happens only in rare large storms, through clay pans and blowholes.
    - The water table is 30 to 120 m deep and almost flat.
    - Mostly **saline**: about 6000 to 15 000 mg/L. Use is a few stock bores.

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightblue}
      The caves formed in a wetter Pliocene climate. Most speleothems are 3 to 5 million years old.

# Fractured rock: Yilgarn, Mount Lofty Ranges, eastern highlands

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{52%}
    - **Yilgarn (WA)**: deeply weathered Archean granite. Saprolite $ K \approx 0.5 $ m/day; water up to more than 250 000 mg/L. Gold mines pump buried palaeochannel sands ($ K $ = 20–70 m/day) with water more than 100 000 years old.
    - **Mount Lofty Ranges (SA)**: fractured metasediments. $ K $ spans seven orders of magnitude. Clearing raised recharge from below 2 to about 60 mm/yr.
    - **Eastern highlands and Tasmania**: low-yield fold-belt rocks used for stock and domestic supply.

  .container width:{48%}
    !(100%)[Mount Lofty salinity](../image/dew_wmlr_fractured_rock_salinity.png "Salinity in fractured-rock wells, Western Mount Lofty Ranges, 2020. SA DEW (2021), CC BY 4.0.")

    .container borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.3cm} background:{.lightred}
      In 2000, 4.4 million ha of the WA Wheatbelt had a high potential to develop dryland salinity.

# Queensland coastal alluvium

.row gap:{1cm}
  .container width:{52%}
    !(100%)[Coastal irrigation salinity](../image/ga_coastal_irrigation_tds.png "Maximum TDS (mg/L) of groundwater in coastal irrigation areas; inset Bundaberg. Ivkovic et al. (2013), GA and NCGRT, CC BY 3.0 AU.")

  .container width:{48%}
    - Shallow, unconfined alluvium and delta sands, pumped for sugarcane.
    - **Burdekin delta**: more than 100 m of sediment. River water has been put back underground since 1965, up to about 45 GL/yr.
    - **Pioneer Valley**: pumping stops when the head in the aquifer above sea level falls below a trigger.
    - **Bundaberg**: about 12 500 ha lost to seawater intrusion since the 1960s.
    - **Lockyer Valley** (inland): use above the sustainable yield; no new bores since 2005.

# Comparing Australian Aquifer Systems (1)

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{95%}
      |**System**|**Aquifer type**|**$ K $ or $ T $**|**Recharge**|**Salinity (mg/L)**|**Main issue**|
      |:---|:---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---|
      |**Great Artesian Basin**|Confined sandstone|$ K $ 0.1–10 m/d|~15 mm/yr|500–1000|Pressure loss|
      |**Murray Basin**|Layered sand, limestone|$ K $ 1–100 m/d|<0.1 to 40 mm/yr|200–300 000|Salinisation|
      |**MDB alluvium**|Sand and gravel|$ T $ to 6500 m²/d|River leakage|<500–10 000|Over-allocation|
      |**Perth Basin**|Sand over sandstone|$ K $ 1–1000 m/d|~120 mm/yr|mostly <1000|Drying climate|
      |**Otway Basin**|Karst over sand|$ T $ to 24 000 m²/d|~1300 GL/yr|<500–10 000|Plantations|
      |**Gippsland Basin**|Sand under coal|$ T $ 530–2600 m²/d|Basin margins|<500–3500|Subsidence|
      |**Newer Volcanics**|Fractured basalt|$ K $ $ 10^{-3} $–$ 10^{2} $ m/d|1–30 mm/yr|<560 to >3000|Wetlands|

      _South and east. Typical ranges from Geoscience Australia, the Bureau of Meteorology and state reports._

# Comparing Australian Aquifer Systems (2)

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{95%}
      |**System**|**Aquifer type**|**$ K $ or $ T $**|**Recharge**|**Salinity (mg/L)**|**Main issue**|
      |:---|:---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---|
      |**NT carbonates**|Karst carbonate|$ T $ to 17 000 m²/d|9–82 mm/yr|~100–2000|River baseflow|
      |**Canning Basin**|Sandstone, artesian|$ K $ 1–140 m/d|0.3–85 mm/yr|<500–5000|Future use|
      |**Pilbara (Millstream)**|Karst dolomite|$ T $ to ~5000 m²/d|Floods|300–1500|Springs, dewatering|
      |**Eucla Basin**|Karst limestone|not measured|Very low|6000–15 000|Saline|
      |**Fractured rock**|Granite, metasediments|$ K $ ~0.5 m/d|<2 to 60 mm/yr|<500 to >250 000|Low yield|
      |**Qld coastal alluvium**|Sand|no data|Rain, river, MAR|<1500 to >6000|Seawater intrusion|

      _North, west and fractured rock. Typical ranges from Geoscience Australia, the Bureau of Meteorology and state reports._

# Summary

.align{center}
  .container width:{75%}
    .box title:{Key takeaways from this lecture:} background:{.lightblue}
      **Aquifer Classification:**
      - Aquifers are the fundamental unit of groundwater hydrology
      - Three types: **unconfined**, **confined**, **perched**—each with distinct boundary conditions
      - Confining beds range from impermeable (aquifuge) to leaky (aquitard), with complex real-world **aquitardifers**

      **Aquifer Properties:**
      - **Transmissivity** ($ T = Kb $) quantifies ease of transmission; **storativity** ($ S $) quantifies storage release
      - Storage mechanisms: elastic ($ S_s $) in confined, gravity drainage ($ S_y $) in unconfined aquifers
      - Relationship: $ n = S_y + S_r $ connects porosity to drainable and retained water

# Summary
.align{center}
  .container width:{75%}
    .box title:{Key takeaways from this lecture:} background:{.lightblue}
      **Real-World Aquifer Systems:**
      - **Alluvial** (Murray–Darling rivers): the most heavily pumped aquifers, recharged mostly by river leakage
      - **Sandstone** (Great Artesian Basin): confined and artesian, with water more than one million years old
      - **Semiconsolidated** (Murray, Gippsland): salinisation, seawater intrusion and land subsidence
      - **Carbonate and karst** (NT, Mount Gambier, Nullarbor): very high transmissivity, springs that keep rivers flowing
      - **Fractured rock** (Yilgarn, Mount Lofty Ranges): about 40% of the stored groundwater, but low yields
      - Climate and land use change recharge: a drying climate in Perth, land clearing in the Mallee
